WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.280
Star Trek creators have proven they know how&nbsp;
to turn on the funny since the beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.640
Whether it's entire episodes, hilarious scenes,&nbsp;
or little more than a couple of lines of dialogue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:14.560
here's what we count as the funniest&nbsp;
moments in the history of Star Trek.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:18.000
One of the most fun conflicts&nbsp;
to witness on Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.520
Deep Space Nine is the back and forth between&nbsp;
Constable Odo and the scheming Ferengi Quark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.840
Perhaps their funniest exchange happens&nbsp;
in Season 4's "The Way of the Warrior,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.040
just before the epic battle between the&nbsp;
eponymous station and a fleet of Klingon ships.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:38.080
Finding Quark outside his bar, Odo tells him&nbsp;
it's time to head to the emergency shelter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.520
but Quark assures Odo he intends to defend his&nbsp;
property. Quark isn't known for being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:49.920
defend himself against so much as a tribble, much&nbsp;
less battle-hardened Klingon warriors, so Odo is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.520
understandably skeptical. The bartender produces&nbsp;
a box that he thinks holds a disrupter pistol.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.480
When he opens the box, boldly claiming he'll "be&nbsp;
ready for" the Klingons, rather than a weapon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.760
there's a note inside. Without skipping a beat or&nbsp;
asking permission, Odo grabs the note and reads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.680
it — it's from Quark's brother Rom, informing him&nbsp;
he gutted his disruptor to fix their replicator.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.920
It's here that Odo hits Quark with a real zinger:

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:15.760
"I will kill him!"

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.040
"With what?"

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:25.200
Deanna Troi's intrusive and flirtatious mother&nbsp;
Lwaxana, played by the late Majel Barrett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:29.440
provides a tremendous amount of&nbsp;
comic relief whenever she shows up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.240
In her third appearance on&nbsp;
Star Trek: The Next Generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.680
her predicament forces Captain Picard to deliver&nbsp;
a speech so ridiculously over-the-top that even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.480
if you've never watched TNG, you've&nbsp;
likely seen the memes inspired by it.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.440
After Will Riker, Deanna, and Lwaxana&nbsp;
are kidnapped by the Ferengi DaiMon Tog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.440
Lwaxana succeeds in getting Riker and&nbsp;
her daughter sent back to the Enterprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.800
by promising to be Tog's lover. When the&nbsp;
Enterprise contacts Tog, Lwaxana pretends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.400
she doesn't want to be rescued, telling&nbsp;
Picard that "it's over" between them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.800
Deanna figures out what her mother is doing&nbsp;
and tells Picard he will have to fight for her.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.960
In a display so melodramatic it would&nbsp;
even make William Shatner groan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.600
Picard professes his love for Lwaxana,&nbsp;
threatens Tog with annihilation, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:19.200
recites famous lines from Shakespearean sonnets&nbsp;
with as much grandiose flair as he can muster.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:27.920
"My love, is a fever, longing still for&nbsp;
that, which longer nurseth the disease."

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.640
Worf watches in disgust, looking&nbsp;
like he's about to rage-puke.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:37.040
It's Picard like you've never seen him —&nbsp;
and probably wouldn't want to see him again.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.480
In Star Trek: The Original Series, the season 2&nbsp;
episode "The Trouble with Tribbles" starts off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.760
on a serious note. The Enterprise is already&nbsp;
on its way to Deep Space Station K-7, but they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.480
put the pedal to the metal when they receive an&nbsp;
alert that their destination is under attack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.120
This alert proves to be a false&nbsp;
alarm, though dangers do await at K-7:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.280
a domineering Federation bureaucrat, a Klingon&nbsp;
spy, and a space trader named Cyrano Jones who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:08.160
sells members of the Enterprise crew some of the&nbsp;
most troublesome pets in the galaxy — tribbles.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.040
"The Trouble with Tribbles" is a classic, and&nbsp;
one of the first examples of a whimsically goofy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:17.760
original series episode that makes a point&nbsp;
of not taking itself too seriously. From the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:22.480
countless puffballs that multiply faster than&nbsp;
a gremlin in a tropical storm to the ridiculous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.680
barroom brawl between the Klingons and Enterprise&nbsp;
crew, to the peddler Cyrano Jones willing to risk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.240
life and limb in order to score a free drink,&nbsp;
"The Trouble with Tribbles" is a great time.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.360
To celebrate his upcoming marriage to&nbsp;
Jadzia in the DS9 episode titled "You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.840
Are Cordially Invited," Worf invites Chief&nbsp;
O'Brien and Doctor Bashir, among others,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.320
to the ritual of Kal'Hyah. Thinking they can&nbsp;
expect the Klingon version of a bachelor party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.640
they happily accept. But rather&nbsp;
than drinking or exotic dancers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.920
the path to Kal'Hyah involves fasting for&nbsp;
four days and nights, being drained of blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.560
hanging from a pole over intense heat,&nbsp;
and other creative forms of torture.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.600
When Worf abruptly cancels the wedding, Bashir and&nbsp;
O'Brien are thrilled to cut their torment short.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.840
They head straight to Quark's for a feast,&nbsp;
but before they can sink their teeth in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.560
Sisko and Martok arrive to let them know&nbsp;
the wedding's back on. When Quark takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:18.960
their untouched food away, you can practically&nbsp;
hear them whining like disappointed puppies.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.360
After several vows from both friends&nbsp;
that they're going to "kill Worf,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.360
the wedding finally arrives. Part of the&nbsp;
friends' duty — as per Klingon tradition — is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:32.800
to attack Worf and Jadzia with blunted weapons&nbsp;
called Ma'Stakas. While everyone else applauds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.280
the newlyweds' kiss, Bashir looks like Obi-Wan&nbsp;
Kenobi waiting for a force field to come down.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:38.500
"Now?"

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.280
"Not yet."

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.480
When Martok finally gives the&nbsp;
word, Bashir and O'Brien rush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.960
Worf and Jadzia with cries of rage and the&nbsp;
credits roll to sounds of the mock battle.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:54.480
One of the best Star Trek films featuring&nbsp;
the original cast is 1986's Star Trek 4:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.840
The Voyage Home. In order to&nbsp;
answer an alien probe's call,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:02.240
which is blindly triggering power losses and&nbsp;
apocalyptic storms on Earth, Kirk and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:08.240
crew must go back in time to recover a humpback&nbsp;
whale — a species extinct by the 23rd century.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:13.840
While original series sent the heroes back in time&nbsp;
on multiple occasions, none of those stories feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:19.600
as natural or believable as in Star Trek 4. Rather&nbsp;
than experiencing any particular physical threat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:23.200
the biggest challenge they face is&nbsp;
culture shock. They don't have money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.440
when they try to use a bus they're foiled by&nbsp;
the need for "exact change," and in particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.720
they're confused by what Spock calls, quote,&nbsp;
"colorful metaphors" — i.e. all the swearing.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:37.680
The best scene involves Kirk and Spock trying&nbsp;
to earn the trust of the marine biologist Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.280
Gillian Taylor. Kirk is attempting, and failing,&nbsp;
to seem like the "normal" one compared to Spock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.880
When Kirk suggests the three of them&nbsp;
get dinner, one of the most natural,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.760
adorably funny moments in the franchise unfolds:

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.840
"You guys like Italian?"

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:52.800
"No."
"Yes."

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:53.200
"Yes."
"No."

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:53.560
"No. "

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:54.060
"Yes."

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:54.800
"No."

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:57.120
"Yes. I love Italian."

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.960
Due to the meddling of the mischievous&nbsp;
entity Q — one of Star Trek's greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.640
villains — Captain Picard, his&nbsp;
senior staff, and Picard's old flame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.040
Vash find themselves transported to&nbsp;
Sherwood Forest in Season 4's "Qpid."

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:15.840
There's little about "Qpid" that won't make you&nbsp;
laugh. Picard and his senior officers are made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:22.320
up as Robin Hood's merry men, including Data, who&nbsp;
bears the famous bald dome of Friar Tuck. Worf is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:27.040
predictably, the most incensed by the&nbsp;
situation, famously complaining to Picard,

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:30.240
"Sir, I protest. I am not a merry man!"

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:36.880
The most laugh-out-loud moment comes as Geordi&nbsp;
rests and plucks at his mandolin. Without comment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:42.640
or permission, Worf calmly walks over, grabs the&nbsp;
instrument, and smashes it against a nearby tree.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:48.080
The Prime Directive prohibits members of Starfleet&nbsp;
from interfering with the natural progression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:53.680
of any society outside the United Federation&nbsp;
of Planets. Serving as a comically dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:59.600
example of why this code exists is the original&nbsp;
series Season 2 episode, "A Piece of the Action."

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:04.280
One hundred years before the events of the&nbsp;
episode, Sigma Iotia two is visited by the Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:10.080
ship Horizon. To help teach the planet's natives,&nbsp;
the Horizon crew leaves behind a number of books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:15.760
including a non-fiction book titled "Chicago&nbsp;
Mobs of the Twenties." After the Horizon leaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:21.360
the Iotians come to see this book as a holy text&nbsp;
and base their entire culture on it. When Kirk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:25.200
Spock, and McCoy beam down to the planet,&nbsp;
they find a world where just about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:29.600
every grown male dresses and speaks&nbsp;
like tough guys in mobster movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:34.560
People openly carry Tommy Guns up and down the&nbsp;
street as if there's nothing strange about it.

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:38.560
"A Piece of the Action" gets even funnier&nbsp;
once Kirk and Spock are outfitted in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:43.280
own mobster get-ups and Kirk attempts&nbsp;
a Chicago gangster impersonation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:47.280
Shatner wouldn't fool anyone on Earth, and&nbsp;
it's tough to tell whether that's intentional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.360
which just makes the whole thing even funnier.

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:54.320
Early in Season 5 of DS9, the series&nbsp;
made television history with "Trials&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:59.040
and Tribble-ations," where, the cast of&nbsp;
the show is sent back in time to the 1967&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:01.920
original series episode&nbsp;
"The Trouble with Tribbles."

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:07.760
This episode uses every opportunity to poke fun&nbsp;
at the franchise. O'Brien, the tireless engineer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:12.560
is so lost on the Enterprise that when he tries to&nbsp;
perform routine maintenance, he takes out all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:18.400
nearby lights. Bashir gets hit on by a woman he's&nbsp;
convinced is his great-grandmother, and afterward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:23.440
he considers the possibility that if he doesn't&nbsp;
get involved with her, he might never be born.

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:28.000
Hands down, the best moment comes when a&nbsp;
waitress points out a table full of Klingons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.840
Everyone but Worf is confused, because,&nbsp;
of course, Klingons look a lot different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:35.200
in the original series. It's the&nbsp;
first time in the franchise anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.840
acknowledges what appears to be a glaring&nbsp;
inconsistency, and it comes off perfectly.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:45.360
"They are Klingons. And it is a long story."

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:49.360
Imagine you're suddenly transported to the&nbsp;
23rd century and you need to use a computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:54.000
more advanced than anything you'd ever&nbsp;
seen. Now consider the opposite scenario,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:56.000
and that's what happens in Star Trek 4.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:01.760
On their quest to bring 20th-century humpback&nbsp;
whales back to the 23rd century, Scotty, McCoy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:07.280
and Sulu are tasked with figuring out a way to&nbsp;
construct a makeshift aquarium aboard the Bounty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:13.200
While Sulu secures an aircraft, Scotty and McCoy&nbsp;
find a plexiglass manufacturer and pretend to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.040
famous scientists with an appointment to&nbsp;
tour the factory. At the end of the tour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.880
Scotty plans to offer the head of the&nbsp;
plant, Dr. Nichols, the futuristic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:26.080
formula for transparent aluminum, but he needs&nbsp;
to use Nichols' computer to prove it can work.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:28.400
Unfortunately, Scotty has a little trouble...

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:28.900
"Computer?"

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:32.320
"Computer!"

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:36.320
Thinking he's figured it out,&nbsp;
McCoy hands Scotty the mouse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:40.560
Smiling widely, Scotty holds the mouse to&nbsp;
his face like a walkie talkie and says,

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:42.160
"Hello, computer."

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:44.640
"Just use the keyboard."

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:50.080
1989's Star Trek 5: The Final Frontier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:55.040
is considered one of the worst films in the&nbsp;
series, though it is not without its merits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:58.640
While it's one of the funnier entries in&nbsp;
the franchise, some of its detractors would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:03.040
argue that the movie's over-reliance&nbsp;
on humor weighs it down. But one easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:08.880
uncharacteristically slapstick moment in Star&nbsp;
Trek 5 remains as an incredibly uproarious gag.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:13.440
After the forces loyal to Spock's fanatical&nbsp;
brother Sybok seize control of the Enterprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:16.640
Kirk, Spock, and Dr. McCoy&nbsp;
are imprisoned in the brig.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:21.280
Scotty soon frees them with explosives,&nbsp;
and the liberated trio makes a plan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:25.680
sneak their way to the emergency transmitter.&nbsp;
When the subject of search parties comes up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:29.520
Scotty gives them directions he's confident&nbsp;
will steer them clear of Sybok's people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:33.680
Before they rush off, Kirk emphatically&nbsp;
thanks Scotty and calls him "amazing."

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:36.080
Turning and walking in the&nbsp;
other direction, Scotty mutters,

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:40.160
"There's nothing amazing about it. I&nbsp;
know this ship like the back of my hand."

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:46.480
If that were true though, he should have&nbsp;
easily been able to avoid that bulkhead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:48.480
It's perfectly timed and classic.

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:50.880
If you understand the context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:55.760
the single-word response Worf delivers to&nbsp;
Quark in the season 4 DS9 episode "Accession"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:00.080
should make you laugh hard enough to knock you&nbsp;
out of any recliner, couch, or captain's chair.

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:03.200
Dr. Bashir and O'Brien are&nbsp;
sharing a drink in Quark's bar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:07.200
discussing the news that O'Brien's wife&nbsp;
Keiko is pregnant with another baby.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:11.760
It's just then that, behind Bashir and&nbsp;
O'Brien, Quark runs into Worf and says,

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.840
"Did you hear? Keiko's&nbsp;
going to have another baby."

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:18.080
To understand what happens next,&nbsp;
you need to know that five years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:22.080
earlier in the Season 5 TNG episode&nbsp;
"Disaster," much of the Enterprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:26.400
is rendered inoperable and most of the crew&nbsp;
is stranded in different parts of the ship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:29.920
This, of course, is when Keiko O'Brien —&nbsp;
pregnant with her first baby — goes into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:34.800
labor while stuck in Ten Forward. As the most&nbsp;
qualified crew member — but still with precious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:39.520
little medical knowledge — Worf is the one&nbsp;
who's forced to assist with Keiko's pregnancy.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:43.840
So this is why, five years later in&nbsp;
"Accession," when Quark delivers the news,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:46.640
Worf's immediate reaction is&nbsp;
to respond with a panicked,

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:47.700
"Now?!?"

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:52.320
Check out one of our newest videos right here!&nbsp;
Plus, even more Looper videos about Star Trek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:57.016
are coming soon. Subscribe to our YouTube channel&nbsp;
and hit the bell so you don't miss a single one.

